summary:
The quota digest (quota_profile_onboarding.json) still holds at 95cf6f86. Credit is taken at the gate, refunded only when success is falsy, and the pill counts lifetime use. This delta covers what the pivot needs.

(a) Auth limits come from slowapi decorators keyed on get_remote_address, with in-memory storage. On Railway that address is a rotating edge peer: on 2026-10-02, 322 anonymous calls came from 155 addresses with no 429. So "5/min per client" really means about 5/min per random edge bucket, and the counts reset on every deploy. The one control that works is the per-email lockout: 5 failures within 900 s gives a 429 ACCOUNT_LOCKED. It fails open without Redis and covers login and PUT email/password only. Register (3/min), social-login (10/min) and refresh (10/min) have no per-email, per-device or per-account control. ENABLE_PROXY_AWARE_RATELIMIT is unsafe as written because it reads the leftmost X-Forwarded-For entry, which the caller controls. The Procfile passes no --forwarded-allow-ips. #299 (a per-user limiter key) is still open.

(b) Metering is by user id only. Redis keys are usage:daily:{uid}:{UTC date} and usage:monthly:{uid}:{UTC month}. The day resets at 00:00 UTC, which is 03:00 in Bahrain/KSA and 04:00 in the UAE. Free accounts get 3/day and 10/month, with the first 3 lifetime-free comparisons counted inside those caps. Premium gets 10/day and 70/month. A Redis error lets the request through (fail-open). Today the device fingerprint only carries lifetime_comparisons_used over at /register, and at /social-login only when ENABLE_SOCIAL_DEVICE_FINGERPRINT is on. The anon:{fp} gate exists but is dark, and with ENABLE_COMPARE_AUTH_REQUIRED on in prod it is mostly unreachable.

(c) What "3 per day per device" needs, none of which exists today:
- a device-keyed daily counter for signed-in users;
- the client sending X-Device-Fingerprint on compare calls (it sends it only on register, social sign-in and share);
- a rule for a missing or invalid header (build 3 would send none);
- a rule for several accounts on one device (each new account gets a fresh 3/day today);
- a premium exemption rule;
- a server-provided reset_at;
- copy fixes on the pill and Paywall.

The fingerprint is a random UUID nonce in SecureStore, plus the bundle id and OS build. The server only checks its format, so a script can send a new hash per request. On Android it resets on uninstall. On iOS it usually survives a reinstall, but Expo says not to rely on that. So a device cap only works on honest clients, and the per-user cap plus the sign-in requirement stay the real spend control.

(d) Login and sign-up limits ride decorators that are always on. The flag set (proxy-aware, default limits, endpoint key) is ordered: proxy-aware must be fixed first. The open items are #299, #114, a per-email register/reset limiter and a verified proxy anchor.

(e) The tests that pin these limits are mostly checks that a string is present in the source, plus a few behaviour tests. Changing TIER_LIMITS breaks the legal-parity pins.
